1	# Contributing to Gemini CLI MCP Server
2	
3	Thank you for your interest in contributing to the Gemini CLI MCP Server! This document provides guidelines and instructions for contributing.
4	
5	## Code of Conduct
6	
7	By participating in this project, you agree to be respectful and constructive in all interactions.
8	
9	## How to Contribute
10	
11	### Reporting Issues
12	
13	- Check if the issue already exists in the [issue tracker](https://github.com/DiversioTeam/gemini-cli-mcp/issues)
14	- Include clear reproduction steps
15	- Provide system information (OS, Python version, Gemini CLI version)
16	- Include relevant error messages and logs
17	
18	### Suggesting Features
19	
20	- Open an issue with the "enhancement" label
21	- Clearly describe the feature and its use case
22	- Be open to discussion about implementation approaches
23	
24	### Submitting Code
25	
26	1. **Fork the Repository**
27	   ```bash
28	   git clone https://github.com/DiversioTeam/gemini-cli-mcp.git
29	   cd gemini-cli-mcp
30	   ```
31	
32	2. **Create a Feature Branch**
33	   ```bash
34	   git checkout -b feature/your-feature-name
35	   ```
36	
37	3. **Set Up Development Environment**
38	   ```bash
39	   uv sync --all-extras --dev
40	   uv run pre-commit install
41	   ```
42	
43	4. **Make Your Changes**
44	   - Follow the existing code style
45	   - Add tests for new functionality
46	   - Update documentation as needed
47	
48	5. **Run Tests and Quality Checks**
49	   ```bash
50	   # Run all tests
51	   uv run pytest
52	
53	   # Check code style
54	   uv run ruff check .
55	   uv run ruff format .
56	
57	   # Type checking
58	   uv run mypy src/
59	
60	   # Security checks
61	   uv run ruff check --select S .
62	   ```
63	
64	6. **Commit Your Changes**
65	   - Use clear, descriptive commit messages
66	   - Follow conventional commit format when possible:
67	     - `feat:` for new features
68	     - `fix:` for bug fixes
69	     - `docs:` for documentation changes
70	     - `test:` for test additions/changes
71	     - `refactor:` for code refactoring
72	
73	7. **Push and Create Pull Request**
74	   ```bash
75	   git push origin feature/your-feature-name
76	   ```
77	   - Open a pull request against the `main` branch
78	   - Fill out the PR template completely
79	   - Link any related issues
80	
81	## Development Guidelines
82	
83	### Code Style
84	
85	- Follow PEP 8 guidelines
86	- Use type hints for all function parameters and returns
87	- Maximum line length: 100 characters
88	- Use meaningful variable and function names
89	
90	### Testing
91	
92	- Write tests for all new functionality
93	- Maintain or improve code coverage (minimum 80%)
94	- Use pytest fixtures for common test setup
95	- Test both success and failure cases
96	
97	### Security
98	
99	- Never commit secrets or credentials
100	- Validate all user inputs
101	- Follow the principle of least privilege
102	- Be mindful of file system access
103	
104	### Documentation
105	
106	- Update README.md for user-facing changes
107	- Add docstrings to all public functions and classes
108	- Include examples in docstrings where helpful
109	- Update type hints as code changes
110	
111	## Review Process
112	
113	1. All PRs require at least one review
114	2. CI checks must pass (tests, linting, type checking)
115	3. Code coverage must not decrease
116	4. Documentation must be updated if needed
117	
118	## Release Process
119	
120	Releases are managed by maintainers and follow semantic versioning:
121	- MAJOR: Breaking changes
122	- MINOR: New features (backward compatible)
123	- PATCH: Bug fixes
124	
125	## Questions?
126	
127	Feel free to open an issue for any questions about contributing!
128	

